<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1556–1627</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan (1556–1627) was a commander and poet at the Mughal court, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s guardian Bairam Khan. As one of Akbar's leading generals he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in 1584 and lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, receiving the submission of the Tarkhan ruler Mirza <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jani-beg">Jani Beg</a> in 1591. He translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>'s memoirs into Persian and is famous for his Hindi couplets (dohas) written under the name Rahim.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdul-rahim-khan-i-khanan.jpg" alt="Abdul Rahim Khan-i-Khanan" width="320" height="571" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hashim · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abd_al-Rahim,_Khan_Khanan_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
